TY  - JOUR
TI  - Cross-system log anomaly detection based on domain adversarial adaptation and batch contrastive learning
AU  - Tang, P.
AU  - Guan, Y.
T2  - Future Generation Computer Systems
AB  - Log anomaly detection is essential for ensuring the stability and security of modern software systems. To overcome the shortage of anomalous logs in newly deployed systems, log-based cross-system anomaly detection methods have emerged, allowing knowledge transfer from data-rich source system logs to these newer systems. However, directly fine-tuning models on small amounts of target system log data often results in overfitting, which compromises the model's generalization ability. Moreover, simply aligning overall feature distributions without aligning class boundaries can lead to feature misalignment across different classes, degrading detection performance. To address these challenges, we propose a cross-system log anomaly detection method named LogDC, which is based on domain adversarial adaptation and batch contrastive learning. Specifically, LogDC combines label classification loss and domain classification loss during training to implement domain adversarial adaptation, which helps the model learn domain-invariant features for universal feature representation across both the source system (source domain) and the target system (target domain). Additionally, batch contrastive learning is introduced to refine class-level feature alignment. This involves minimizing the feature distance between same-class samples from the source and target domains within a batch, while maximizing the distance between samples of different classes. Through this dual approach, LogDC effectively transfers and utilizes the anomaly detection capabilities of the source domain to the target domain. Experimental results demonstrate that LogDC achieves superior performance compared to existing methods on public datasets. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
