TY  - JOUR
TI  - MAGE: A structural-prior-guided graph-language multimodal framework for business process anomaly detection
AU  - Yang, Q.
AU  - Lu, K.
AU  - Fang, X.
T2  - Information Systems
AB  - Business process event logs record activities, resources, timestamps, and other process attributes, and therefore provide the basis for trace-level anomaly detection. Existing methods often lack a unified reference for normal behavior and do not explicitly coordinate structural deviations with semantic information. We propose MAGE, a structural-prior-guided graph-enhanced language embedding framework for business process anomaly detection. MAGE first constructs structural priors solely from normal traces in the training set, including normal directly-follows relations, trace-length statistics, and adjacent-event resource-switching statistics. It then compares each trace with these priors to generate structural deviation signals and constructs both an activity-resource-time-slice heterogeneous graph and a structure-aware textual representation. A heterogeneous graph Transformer extracts structural representations, while a LoRA-adapted Qwen3 model extracts semantic representations. Gated cross-attention fuses the two modalities for trace-level anomaly classification. The structural prior and graph self-supervised pretraining use only normal training traces, whereas the final multimodal classifier is jointly optimized on labeled training samples. Experiments on synthetic logs and public real event logs with controlled anomaly injection show that MAGE achieves the highest mean F1-scores of 0.872 and 0.860, respectively, among the compared methods under the evaluated settings. Ablation results further indicate that the structural prior, heterogeneous graph encoding, structure-aware prompting, and cross-modal fusion each contribute positively to F1-score under the evaluated settings. Together, these findings provide evidence for combining normal-process structural constraints with language-based semantic information in trace-level business process anomaly detection. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
